---
title: skyBlip Go
nav_title: skyBlip Go
description: The pocket tracker, screen by screen · the traffic plot, the alarm that says where to look, the four pages, the menus, and what the e-paper and the battery are protected from.
---

<section id="simulator">
  <h2 class="kicker">skyBlip Go</h2>
  <h3>Switch one on. It runs the firmware a real one runs.</h3>

  <%= render "shared/simulator",
        wake: "Hold the lower key to switch it back on.",
        traffic: "Put an aircraft in the sky",
        keys: "Once it is on, the keyboard flies it, and T puts another aircraft in the sky.",
        on: "running",
        off: "off",
        menu: "menu",
        glass: "The simulated e-paper panel of a skyBlip Go",
        key: "The device button: press to open the menu of the page you are on, hold two seconds to switch off, hold it again to switch on",
        pad: "The capacitive pad: tap to change page, hold one second to come back to the radar",
        reset: "RST: reboot the device",
        caption: "Sensors are simulated, the firmware is not. It is the build this site was published from.",
        missing: "The simulator is not part of this build of the site." %>

  <p class="note">
    Nothing is flying out there until you ask for one, and each aircraft the
    button adds broadcasts real ADS-L frames that the firmware receives, decodes
    and plots. The lamp on the left of the glass is the firmware&rsquo;s, lit
    while a contact is worth an alarm. The one on the right belongs to the
    charger, so on this page it stays dark. Every screen below is a capture of
    this same firmware.
  </p>
</section>

<section>
  <h2 class="kicker">What it shows you</h2>

  <div class="feature">
    <div>
      <h3>A plot you read <span class="highlight">like a window</span>.</h3>
      <p>
        Your aircraft is in the middle, nose up, and it never turns: the sky
        turns around it. A target drawn to the upper right is a target to look
        for off the right of the nose, and nothing at all is written across the
        top of the glass, because that is the half you are flying into.
      </p>
      <p>
        Each aircraft is a cut stone. A diamond is traffic at your level, the top
        half on its own is one above you, the bottom half one below, so the mark
        says where to look before it is read. A caret rides a target that is climbing
        or sinking, and the line out of it is where it will be in a minute.
      </p>
    </div>
    <figure class="shot">
      <%= pages_image_tag "radar.png", alt: "The radar page: three aircraft drawn as cut stones, one above, one below and one level and climbing, each with its one-minute leader line, own ship's dashed run with a ball at each of the next two minutes, the four-mile ring, the flight time and the count of aircraft in the ring" %>
      <figcaption>Radar</figcaption>
    </figure>
  </div>

  <ul class="points">
    <li>Heading up, never north up</li>
    <li>Ring of 1, 2, 4 or 8&nbsp;NM</li>
    <li>A stone cut to say above, level or below</li>
    <li>A minute of track ahead of each target</li>
    <li>Aircraft counted inside the ring</li>
    <li>Time since takeoff, for the logbook</li>
  </ul>

  <div class="feature feature--reverse">
    <div>
      <h3>One alarm, and what it says is <span class="highlight">where to look</span>.</h3>
      <p>
        An aircraft inside 3&nbsp;km and 300&nbsp;m of you fills its stone, and
        the quadrant it sits in flashes once a second. A quarter of the screen
        going black and white is seen without being looked at, which is the
        whole instruction: turn your head that way.
      </p>
      <p>
        There is nothing above it and there never will be. An instruction to
        climb or descend has to be coordinated with the other aircraft, and this
        device has no link to coordinate over. The loudest thing it can say is
        look.
      </p>
    </div>
    <figure class="shot">
      <%= pages_image_tag "alarm.png", alt: "The radar page during a traffic advisory: one quadrant of the screen inverted to black, with the filled stone of the converging aircraft and its leader line reversed out of it" %>
      <figcaption>Traffic advisory</figcaption>
    </figure>
  </div>

  <ul class="points">
    <li>3&nbsp;km around you, 300&nbsp;m above and below</li>
    <li>The sector flashes at one flip a second</li>
    <li>Buzzer, vibration and lamp together</li>
    <li>Any page you are on gives way to the radar</li>
    <li>One touch of the pad silences it</li>
    <li>A caution, never an instruction</li>
  </ul>

  <div class="feature">
    <div>
      <h3>Who is out there, <span class="highlight">nearest first</span>.</h3>
      <p>
        The radar answers where to look. This page answers who that is: one row
        per aircraft the device can place, the ground distance to it, and how far
        above or below you it is. Your own address and the count of everything
        heard stand on the top line.
      </p>
      <p>
        The letter is where the report came from. <span class="mono">A</span> is
        a direct ADS-L frame, <span class="mono">F</span> is FLARM-compatible
        traffic, <span class="mono">U</span> is an aircraft relayed by a ground
        station.
      </p>
    </div>
    <figure class="shot">
      <%= pages_image_tag "nearby.png", alt: "The nearby page: own address and the count of everything heard on the top line, then five aircraft listed nearest first, each with its source letter, its address, its ground distance and its relative altitude" %>
      <figcaption>Nearby</figcaption>
    </figure>
  </div>

  <div class="feature feature--reverse">
    <div>
      <h3>Six dials, and <span class="highlight">the clock you copy</span>.</h3>
      <p>
        Ground speed, horizon, altimeter, turn and slip, track, vertical speed.
        The figure over the middle is the time since takeoff, which is the one
        number on this device that ends up in a logbook.
      </p>
      <p>
        The horizon pitches with the flight path and rolls with the bank a
        coordinated turn at that speed would need, because nothing on the board
        measures a bank. The ball is measured, and it answers the rudder. Nothing
        here is certified, and none of it is an attitude indicator to fly in
        cloud.
      </p>
    </div>
    <figure class="shot">
      <%= pages_image_tag "sixpack.png", alt: "The six-pack page: ground speed, artificial horizon with the flight time above it, altimeter, turn coordinator with its ball, track card and vertical speed" %>
      <figcaption>Six-pack</figcaption>
    </figure>
  </div>

  <div class="feature">
    <div>
      <h3>What the airframe <span class="highlight">has pulled</span>.</h3>
      <p>
        The fourth page is the accelerometer read as an accelerometer, with
        nothing inferred from it. The marker is where the wing is now, the box
        around it is where it has been: the most and least it has pulled, and how
        far it has been thrown to each hand.
      </p>
      <p>
        The peaks belong to this flight, and they reset when the clock starts.
        Fore and aft has a strip of its own, because braking and a rough landing
        are not a wing load.
      </p>
    </div>
    <figure class="shot">
      <%= pages_image_tag "gmeter.png", alt: "The g-meter page: the normal and lateral load as a marker in a field with the box of this flight's peaks around it, a fore-and-aft strip beside it, and three rows of figures in tenths of a g" %>
      <figcaption>G-meter</figcaption>
    </figure>
  </div>
</section>

<section>
  <h2 class="kicker">How you work it</h2>

  <div class="feature">
    <div>
      <h3>The pad moves, <span class="highlight">the button acts</span>.</h3>
      <p>
        Two contacts, and that split is the whole vocabulary. The capacitive pad
        on the top edge walks the four pages and can change nothing. The button
        on the side opens the menu of the page you are standing on, and every
        further press steps the row you are on.
      </p>
      <p>
        Two pages have one. Every setting a thumb can change is under the radar,
        the first screen a device is set up on, and the list&rsquo;s menu opens
        the pages that are off the walk. Nothing is a draft, each press is one
        finished change.
      </p>
    </div>
    <figure class="shot">
      <%= pages_image_tag "menu-radar.png", alt: "The radar menu: aircraft type, units, range, alarm and volume, with the hint PAD MOVES - BUTTON CHANGES at the foot" %>
      <figcaption>The radar menu</figcaption>
    </figure>
  </div>

  <ul class="points">
    <li>A tap of the pad: the next page</li>
    <li>A touch of a second: back to the radar</li>
    <li>A press: the menu, where the page has one</li>
    <li>Presses after that: step the row</li>
    <li>Past the last row: back to the picture</li>
    <li>A minute untouched: back on its own</li>
  </ul>

  <div class="feature feature--reverse">
    <div>
      <h3>Four pages on the walk, <span class="highlight">the rest by name</span>.</h3>
      <p>
        A tap moves between the radar, the list, the instruments and the g-meter,
        and that is all it moves between: a pilot cycling the glass in flight
        should never land on the satellite bars. A board with no inertial sensor
        has no g-meter to draw, so the tap steps over it rather than offering a
        picture of <span class="mono">NO SENSOR</span> every fourth time.
      </p>
      <p>
        Everything else is opened from the menu of the list, by name, and hands
        you back where you were: the four pages below are the bench&rsquo;s, the
        apron&rsquo;s and the bug report&rsquo;s, and none of them is a picture to
        land on with a thumb.
      </p>
    </div>
    <figure class="shot">
      <%= pages_image_tag "menu-nearby.png", alt: "The nearby menu: radio log, satellites, status and self test, the four pages opened by name" %>
      <figcaption>The list&rsquo;s menu</figcaption>
    </figure>
  </div>

  <div class="shots">
    <figure class="shot">
      <%= pages_image_tag "status.png", alt: "The status page: fix and satellites, position, track, traffic count, transmit state, the sensor hub, pressure, the two altitudes, speed, vertical speed and cell voltage" %>
      <figcaption>Status</figcaption>
    </figure>
    <figure class="shot">
      <%= pages_image_tag "satellites.png", alt: "The satellites page: one bar per satellite grouped by constellation, against a dotted line at 40 dB-Hz" %>
      <figcaption>Satellites</figcaption>
    </figure>
    <figure class="shot">
      <%= pages_image_tag "radio-log.png", alt: "The radio log: one row per burst heard or sent, newest first, with its time, direction, channel, address and level" %>
      <figcaption>Radio log</figcaption>
    </figure>
    <figure class="shot">
      <%= pages_image_tag "self-test.png", alt: "The self test page: one row per part of the board, each with the part that answered and its verdict" %>
      <figcaption>Self test</figcaption>
    </figure>
  </div>

  <div class="table-scroll">
    <table>
      <thead>
        <tr><th>Gesture</th><th>On a page</th><th>In a menu</th></tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr>
          <th>Tap the pad</th>
          <td>the next page</td>
          <td>the next row, and off the last row the page it belongs to</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <th>Touch the pad, 1&nbsp;s</th>
          <td>silences a standing alarm, and with none standing, the radar</td>
          <td>the radar</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <th>One press of the button</th>
          <td>opens this page&rsquo;s menu, or refuses a question on the glass</td>
          <td>acts on the focused row, and every further press steps it again</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <th>Two presses, under 0.6&nbsp;s apart</th>
          <td colspan="2">allows the question on the glass, and means nothing when none stands</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <th>Hold the button, 2&nbsp;s</th>
          <td colspan="2">switches the device off</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <th>The same hold, finger on the pad</th>
          <td colspan="2">switches it off and leaves the screen blank, for storage</td>
        </tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>
</section>

<section>
  <h2 class="kicker">On the apron, and in the bag</h2>

  <div class="feature">
    <div>
      <h3>It says <span class="highlight">what it is waiting for</span>.</h3>
      <p>
        One press and it is on, straight to the traffic picture. With no
        position yet the ring says so, and the word under it is how far the
        receiver has got: a receiver that has gone quiet, solutions arriving with
        no date on them yet, or a date read off a satellite.
      </p>
      <p>
        That is the difference between a device to wait for and a device to
        carry into the open. The first fix announces itself, and the picture
        becomes a picture of the sky.
      </p>
    </div>
    <figure class="shot">
      <%= pages_image_tag "no-fix.png", alt: "The radar page with no position: the empty ring carries the words NO FIX and, under them, how far the receiver has got" %>
      <figcaption>Waiting for the sky</figcaption>
    </figure>
  </div>

  <div class="feature feature--reverse">
    <div>
      <h3>The wordmark on the glass <span class="highlight">means off</span>.</h3>
      <p>
        A hold of two seconds switches it off: the radio is parked, the panel is
        given three seconds to draw the wordmark, and the rails drop once your
        thumb is off the button. E-paper keeps its image with no power at all,
        so that is what an off device wears.
      </p>
      <p>
        The same hold with a finger on the pad leaves the glass blank instead.
        One image left on an e-paper for a winter leaves a shadow the panel
        never fully loses, and a device going into a cupboard should carry
        nothing.
      </p>
    </div>
    <figure class="shot">
      <%= pages_image_tag "off.png", alt: "The parked screen of a device that has been switched off: the skyBlip wordmark, held by the e-paper with no power" %>
      <figcaption>Off</figcaption>
    </figure>
  </div>

  <div class="table-scroll">
    <table>
      <thead>
        <tr><th>From off</th><th>What happens</th></tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr><th>Press the button</th><td>boots, unless the cell is under 3.4&nbsp;V with no charger</td></tr>
        <tr><th>Plug the charger in</th><td>charges, stays off: a cable in a flight bag is not a request to transmit</td></tr>
        <tr><th>Hold the button, plug the charger in</th><td>boots and charges</td></tr>
        <tr><th>Press after a flat-battery shutdown</th><td>nothing, by design: plug the charger in</td></tr>
        <tr><th>Press <span class="mono">RST</span></th><td>reboots, and twice inside half a second, the USB bootloader</td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>
</section>

<section>
  <h2 class="kicker">With your phone</h2>

  <div class="feature">
    <div>
      <h3>It appears by name, and <span class="highlight">three screens</span> read it at once.</h3>
      <p>
        <span class="mono">skyBlip Go 5B5AFE</span>: the product, then its own
        24-bit address. Nothing to pair, no code to type. It carries the FLARM
        NMEA stream on both of the Bluetooth serial profiles navigation software
        looks for, so the app you already fly with finds it.
      </p>
      <p>
        The tablet on the yoke, the phone in a pocket and the passenger&rsquo;s
        iPad all read the same traffic. Configuration is the one thing that is
        not shared: the first app to write keeps that right until it
        disconnects, and the second is told who holds it.
      </p>
    </div>
    <figure class="shot shot--photo">
      <%= image_tag "pages/index/skyblip-go-techo.jpg", alt: "A skyBlip Go, white case with its antenna, the wordmark on the e-paper screen" %>
    </figure>
  </div>

  <ul class="points">
    <li>No pairing, no code, no account</li>
    <li>Three connections at once</li>
    <li>Nordic UART and FLARM-style profiles</li>
    <li>SDVFR Next, SkyDemon, ForeFlight and the rest</li>
    <li>Up to sixteen flights kept on the device</li>
    <li>Collected over the same link on the ground</li>
  </ul>

  <h3>Two presses allow, one press refuses.</h3>
  <p class="lede">
    When an app asks for something that could change the aircraft you are flying,
    new firmware, a settings write, erasing your flights, switching the device
    off, the device does not act on the phone&rsquo;s word. It puts the question
    on its own glass, names it, and waits thirty seconds:
    <span class="mono">PRESS TWICE TO ALLOW</span>,
    <span class="mono">ONE PRESS REFUSES</span>. No answer inside the window is a
    refusal too.
  </p>
  <p class="lede">
    Presses only count once the question has reached the glass and your thumb has
    stopped, so a run of presses that was stepping a value cannot be spent on an
    authorisation. The pad is inert while a question stands.
  </p>
</section>

<section>
  <h2 class="kicker">Technical notes</h2>

  <h3>Why the screen behaves the way it does.</h3>
  <p class="lede">
    The panel is a 1.54&Prime; e-paper, 200&times;200 pixels. It holds its last
    image with the rails down and draws nothing to keep it there, which is what
    makes the parked wordmark an indicator and a blank screen the right thing to
    store.
  </p>
  <p class="lede">
    Every frame in flight is a partial refresh. The full waveform, the one that
    flashes the panel black and white for a second and a half, is spent twice in
    a session: at power on, and at power off. A page change goes through a
    single black wipe instead, and the alarm&rsquo;s one flip a second is the
    panel&rsquo;s own floor rather than a rate we picked. Faster is possible, and
    a large black region toggled that hard is ghosting the glass never loses.
  </p>

  <ul class="points">
    <li>Partial refresh on every frame</li>
    <li>The full waveform twice a session</li>
    <li>A page change is one black wipe, a third of a second</li>
    <li>A frame identical to the last is never sent</li>
    <li>Left alone above 50&nbsp;&deg;C, its maker&rsquo;s limit</li>
    <li>Parked white at the cutoff, never mid-picture</li>
  </ul>

  <h3>Why the cell lasts the day.</h3>
  <p class="lede">
    Switching off is a sequence, not a rail drop, and the order is the content of
    it. The external flash is told to sleep while it still has a supply to hear
    the command: after the rail that command does nothing, and the part draws its
    full standby current for the whole night the device spends in a bag looking
    switched off.
  </p>
  <p class="lede">
    The rest is the radio and the sensors. Airborne the device speaks once a
    second, parked once every ten, and which of the ten seconds is its own comes
    from its address, so a field full of aircraft spreads itself out.
  </p>

  <ul class="points">
    <li>Radio asleep before its reset line</li>
    <li>Flash, receiver, rails, pins, in that order</li>
    <li>1&nbsp;Hz in the air, 0.1&nbsp;Hz on the ground</li>
    <li>The hub&rsquo;s gyroscope is never read: it costs a milliamp</li>
    <li>Charging refused outside 5 to 60&nbsp;&deg;C</li>
    <li>The lamp winks, and is never held solid</li>
  </ul>

  <h3>What the gauge will not do.</h3>
  <p class="lede">
    It reads two curves, not one. On a charger the terminal sits above the
    cell&rsquo;s own voltage, so the same reading means a far emptier cell, and
    the percentage says which curve it read.
  </p>
  <p class="lede">
    It never walks backwards, and a 22&nbsp;dBm burst sagging the rail does not
    move it. A gauge that jumps when the radio keys is a gauge a pilot stops
    believing.
  </p>

  <div class="table-scroll">
    <table class="modules">
      <thead>
        <tr><th>Threshold</th><th>What it does</th></tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr><th>3.50&nbsp;V</th><td>the warning: from here nothing durable is written except the flight record, which is the evidence of the flight that is ending</td></tr>
        <tr><th>3.40&nbsp;V</th><td>a boot on a cell this flat is refused, and the button is left unarmed so a bag cannot keep asking</td></tr>
        <tr><th>3.20&nbsp;V</th><td>the cutoff: the device switches itself off, on three consecutive samples, so a transmit sag cannot act</td></tr>
        <tr><th>1.80&nbsp;V</th><td>below this the reading is a floating divider, not a flat cell, and nothing acts on it</td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>

  <h3>Why there is no pairing.</h3>
  <p class="lede">
    Turning Bluetooth pairing on encrypts the GATT characteristics, which is a
    known Web Bluetooth failure on Windows and would break the browser page that
    installs firmware. So the boundary is the image and not the link.
  </p>

  <ul class="points">
    <li>The bootloader verifies a signature over our key</li>
    <li>An older version than the installed one is refused</li>
    <li>An open transport cannot install firmware</li>
    <li>Anything else it could ask for is ground-only</li>
    <li>Refused unless it is confirmed on the glass</li>
    <li>Physical presence, where a pairing code would be</li>
  </ul>
</section>

